% ================== ABSTRACT & KEYWORDS ==================
\section*{Abstract}

This project represents the third stage of the Project Gutenberg Book Search Engine.  
It focuses on building a scalable cluster-oriented design using Docker, Kubernetes, and distributed microservices. A key highlight is the implementation of a highly available ActiveMQ Artemis cluster for robust message handling.

\section*{Keywords}

Docker, Kubernetes, Cluster, Microservices, ActiveMQ Artemis, High Availability, kind.

\newpage
\tableofcontents
\newpage

% ================== SECTIONS ==================
\section{Introduction}

In this stage, the focus has shifted towards creating a robust, distributed architecture capable of handling large-scale data processing for a search engine. We maintain the core microservices (Ingestion, Indexing, Search) but deploy them in a clustered environment ensuring scalability and fault tolerance.

\section{System Architecture}

This project implements a distributed architecture designed for scalability and fault tolerance.

\subsection{Distributed Messaging: ActiveMQ Artemis}

A core component of our architecture is the message broker. We have migrated to using \textbf{ActiveMQ Artemis} configured as a high-availability cluster.
We have deployed a 3-node ActiveMQ Artemis cluster. The nodes (`node1`, `node2`, `node3`) work together to form a distributed messaging fabric. \textbf{ActiveMQ Artemis} was chosen for its high performance and advanced features, as detailed in the official documentation [3].  
This setup provides several key advantages:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{High Efficiency and Load Distribution}: By redistributing messages across the cluster, we ensure that no single broker is a bottleneck. Consumers can connect to any node in the cluster, and messages are efficiently routed to where they are needed. This redistribution mechanism optimizes the consumption of messages by the worker machines (Ingestion and Indexing services).
    
    \item \textbf{Redistribution Configuration}: The key mechanism enabling this distributed behavior is the \texttt{redistribution-delay} setting within the \texttt{broker.xml} configuration files. By setting this parameter to \texttt{0} inside the \texttt{<address-settings>} block, we ensure that messages are immediately redistributed to other nodes if there are no local consumers available, maximizing throughput and minimizing latency.
    
    \item \textbf{High Availability and Fault Tolerance}: The cluster architecture avoids a \textit{Single Point of Failure (SPOF)}. If one machine or broker node goes down, the other nodes continue to operate, ensuring the workflow is not interrupted. The system is highly distributed, meaning the failure of a single component does not stop the entire data processing pipeline.
\end{enumerate}

The services connect to the broker using a failover transport string, enabling them to automatically reconnect to available nodes in case of network issues or node failure.

\subsection{Data Management: Hazelcast and MongoDB}

To optimize performance without sacrificing data integrity, we implemented a hybrid data strategy:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Hazelcast (Distributed Caching)}: We utilize \textbf{Hazelcast} as an in-memory data grid. It acts as a distributed cache to store inverted index fragments during the heavy indexing process. This allows for extremely fast read/write operations (low latency) that are essential for the performance of the search engine.
    
    \item \textbf{MongoDB (Persistence Layer)}: It is important to note that Hazelcast \textbf{does not replace} MongoDB. \textbf{MongoDB} remains the authoritative source of truth, providing persistent storage for the book references and metadata. While Hazelcast offers speed for active processing, MongoDB ensures that data is durably stored and preserved across system restarts or failures.
\end{itemize}

\section{Microservices Architecture}

The system relies on three main microservices, all containerized and orchestrated:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Ingestion Service}: Responsible for downloading books from Project Gutenberg. It communicates with the broker to receive commands and publishes status updates.
    \item \textbf{Indexing Service}: Consumes book data and builds an inverted index. It uses a distributed map (Hazelcast) to store terms and occurrences effectively across the cluster.
    \item \textbf{Search Service}: Provides the user interface and API for querying the indexed books.
\end{itemize}

\section{Background and Investigation Work}

\subsection{Kubernetes: Local Cluster Deployment with kind}

As part of the investigation, Kubernetes was studied using the official kind documentation [1], and the internal architecture of Kubernetes was analyzed through the official project documentation [2].  
A significant effort was dedicated to understanding and configuring Kubernetes using \texttt{kind} (Kubernetes IN Docker) [1]. Although Kubernetes was not required for the final deployment of the microservices, it was intentionally explored due to its relevance in large-scale distributed architectures.

This research complements the main system development by offering insights into how the ingestion, indexing, and search services could operate inside a realistic distributed environment. Kubernetes provides features such as declarative deployments, load balancing, automatic recovery, and namespace isolation.

\subsubsection*{Overview and Motivation}

Docker provides lightweight containers, but Kubernetes extends these capabilities to full orchestration. Benefits include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Automated Pod recovery.
    \item Declarative configuration with YAML.
    \item Load balancing with Services.
    \item Autoscaling capabilities.
    \item Namespaces for isolation.
    \item Multi-node cluster simulation with \texttt{kind}.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Project Structure and Manifests}

The Kubernetes configuration used during the investigation was organized in \texttt{cluster/manifests}, divided per microservice:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{indexing/}
    \item \texttt{ingestion/}
    \item \texttt{search/}
\end{itemize}

Each included:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Namespace  
    \item Deployment  
    \item Service
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Namespaces}

\begin{lstlisting}
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: indexing
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Deployments}

\begin{lstlisting}
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: indexing
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: indexing
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: indexing
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: indexing
        image: localhost:5001/indexing:0.0.1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Services}

\begin{lstlisting}
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: indexing
spec:
  selector:
    app: indexing
  ports:
  - protocol: TCP
    port: 80
    targetPort: 8080
  type: ClusterIP
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Cluster Creation with kind}

\begin{lstlisting}
kind: Cluster
apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4
nodes:
  - role: control-plane
  - role: worker
  - role: worker
  - role: worker
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}
kind create cluster --config=cluster/cluster-config.yaml
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Local Docker Registry Integration}

\begin{lstlisting}
docker build -t localhost:5001/mi-app:latest .
docker push localhost:5001/mi-app:latest
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Verification and Cleanup}

\begin{lstlisting}
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A
kind delete cluster
docker rm -f kind-registry
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Conclusion}

This Kubernetes investigation provides a foundation for future scalability and real-world deployment strategies.
% ================== DOCKER HUB SECTION ==================
\subsection{Container Preparation and Registry Publishing for Project Deployment}

To ensure consistent execution across different laboratory machines and hardware architectures, 
all microservice images were built using \texttt{docker buildx} with multi-architecture support.  
Specifically, the \texttt{--platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64} flag was used to generate 
cross-platform images that run seamlessly on both standard x86\_64 servers 
and ARM-based systems (e.g., Apple Silicon).  
This approach prevents compatibility issues such as 
``no matching manifest for linux/amd64'' that may occur when building locally on ARM devices.

All images were then published publicly to Docker Hub under the account 
\textbf{giselabcr8888} using the \texttt{--push} flag. This ensures that both architectures, 
along with their manifest list, are available for transparent pull operations on any machine.

The repository is accessible at:

\begin{center}
\textbf{\url{https://hub.docker.com/repositories/giselabcr8888}}
\end{center}

\subsubsection{Authentication}

\begin{verbatim}
docker login
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Multi-Architecture Image Building and Publishing}

The following commands were used to build and publish each service image:

\subsubsection*{Ingestion Service}

\begin{verbatim}
docker buildx build \
  --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \
  -t giselabcr8888/ingestion:multi \
  --push \
  ./ingestion
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection*{Indexing Service}

\begin{verbatim}
docker buildx build \
  --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \
  -t giselabcr8888/indexing:multi \
  --push \
  ./indexing
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection*{Search Service}

\begin{verbatim}
docker buildx build \
  --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \
  -t giselabcr8888/search:multi \
  --push \
  ./search
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Benefits of Using Multi-Architecture Publishing}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Ensures reproducibility across all laboratory environments.
    \item Avoids the need to rebuild images in each machine.
    \item Guarantees compatibility with both AMD64 and ARM64 architectures.
    \item Provides a single, validated, publicly available image source for deployment.
\end{itemize}

\section{Testing and Verification}

To ensure the reliability, correctness, and performance of the distributed system, a comprehensive testing strategy was implemented. This includes unit tests, acceptance tests, and performance benchmarks.

\subsection{Unit Testing}
Unit tests were essential for verifying the core logic of individual components in isolation.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{TextTokenizerTest}: Verifies that the book content is correctly split into tokens (terms) for indexing. This is critical for the accuracy of the search engine.
    \item \textbf{GutenbergHeaderSerializerTest}: Ensures that metadata (title, author, language) is correctly extracted from the raw Project Gutenberg files.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Integration and Acceptance Testing}
To guarantee that the microservices interact correctly, acceptance tests were employed.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{IndexingApiAcceptanceTest}: Validates the end-to-end functionality of the indexing service, ensuring that books sent to the API are correctly processed and stored.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Performance Benchmarking}
Given the "Big Data" nature of the project, performance verification was paramount. We used the \textbf{JMH (Java Microbenchmark Harness)} framework to rigorously test the system's throughput and latency.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{IngestionBenchmarks}: Measured the throughput of the ingestion process, ensuring the system can handle high volumes of book data.
    \item \textbf{SearchBenchmarks}: Tested the search latency under load, simulating concurrent user queries to verify the responsiveness of the cache and database.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Manual Verification}
In addition to automated tests, scripts such as \texttt{verify\_hazelcast.sh} were created to manually verify the connectivity and operation of the distributed components (e.g., the Hazelcast cache) within the Docker container environment.

